{"id": "Dd2UVU_sVNH", "title": "Video by arnitly", "description": "On September 23, Stanford and NVIDIA released CLM-8B, a Contrastive Language Model. Like Jev, it doesn\u2019t generate text. It turns the situation and each possible action into vectors and picks the closest match. Under the hood it is a frozen Qwen3-8B with a small 20M-parameter layer trained on top.\n\nThe speed comes from caching. When the list of actions stays the same, like tools in an agent or buttons in a game, CLM encodes them once and only re-reads the new state. The team reports up to 9x faster than Jev on computer-use, gaming and tool-calling, and 13x at around 1,000 options.\n\nOn coding, Opus 5 and Fable 5 wrote several attempts per task and each model picked one. CLM\u2019s picks scored 81.6% on DeepSWE and 87.6% on Terminal-Bench 2.1, against 71.1% and 83.1% for Jev. The team also says Jev did worse than random selection on these long tasks. All of this is self-reported on 38 and 30 held-out tasks, with no outside replication yet.\n\nWeights, data and code are open under Apache 2.0, and a multimodal 35B version is due next month.", "channel": "arnitly", "uploader_id": "78156213250", "uploader": "Arnav Jaitly", "timestamp": 1790636576, "like_count": 1657, "comment_count": 32, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/150.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode": "navigate", "Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1389707369897499a", "manifest_url": null, "ext": "m4a", "width": null, "height": null, "tbr": 54.942, "asr": 44100, "fps": null, "language": null, "format_note": "DASH audio", "filesize": null, "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "dynamic_range": null, "url": "https://scontent-sjc6-1.cdninstagram.com/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQP73j7geZ26N